  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How are offshore turbines connected together?",
      "a": "Array cables buried in the seabed link the turbines in strings, usually at about 33,000 or 66,000 volts. Each string runs back to the offshore substation.",
      "asks": [
        "What are array cables?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does an offshore substation do?",
      "a": "It collects the power from all the array cables and uses big transformers to step up the voltage, so the long trip to shore wastes less energy. It sits on a steel platform standing on the seabed.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a wind farm need a substation at sea?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the export cable carry power under the sea?",
      "a": "The export cable has thick copper or aluminium cores wrapped in insulation and steel armour. It is buried a metre or more under the seabed to protect it from anchors and fishing nets.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a subsea power cable?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do some wind farms use direct current cables?",
      "a": "A long alternating current cable under the sea wastes a lot of current just charging and discharging the cable every cycle. Over roughly 80 kilometres or more, high-voltage direct current loses less, so converter stations change AC to DC and back.",
      "asks": [
        "What is HVDC?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens when a wind farm makes more than the grid needs?",
      "a": "The grid operator can ask the wind farm to turn some turbines down, called curtailment. Or the spare power can charge batteries or be sent abroad through interconnectors.",
      "asks": [
        "What is curtailment?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Hornsea 2, off the Yorkshire coast, has a capacity of about 1.3 gigawatts and was the world's largest offshore wind farm when completed in 2022.",
    "Offshore wind farms far from shore often use high-voltage direct current links to cut losses."
  ],
